# Grade 12 (part b): teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'12-4-1': dict(
  saint="Elizabeth: the first person to call Mary’s unborn child Lord.",
  tryit=["List what science says about early human development.", "List what philosophy asks.", "State the Church’s conclusion and its basis."],
  saintStory="Elizabeth was the wife of the priest Zechariah and a relative of Mary. Both were righteous before God, but they had no child and were old. In the Temple, Zechariah was told by the angel Gabriel that Elizabeth would bear a son, to be named John, who would prepare the way for the Lord. Zechariah doubted and was struck silent. Elizabeth conceived and hid herself for five months, saying, “Thus the Lord has done to me in the days when he looked on me, to take away my reproach among men.”\nIn her sixth month, Mary, who had just conceived by the Holy Spirit, traveled to the hill country of Judea to visit her. When Mary greeted her, the child in Elizabeth’s womb leaped, and Elizabeth was filled with the Holy Spirit. She cried out, “Blessed are you among women, and blessed is the fruit of your womb! And why is this granted me, that the mother of my Lord should come to me?”\nBefore any ultrasound, Elizabeth recognized an unborn child as “my Lord” and her own child as responding with joy. The Church sees in this scene the dignity of life in the womb.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “At what point does a human life begin? What facts do we know? What further questions are philosophical rather than scientific?” Separate the answers into two columns on the board.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church’s teaching on the sanctity of life is rooted in Scripture, reason and tradition. Scripture: ‘You shall not kill’ (Exodus 20:13); God knows us in the womb (Jeremiah 1:5, Psalm 139:13-16); Elizabeth’s encounter (Luke 1:39-45); the Incarnation itself, where the Son of God began as an embryo. Tradition: the Didache and Letter of Barnabas (first century) forbid abortion; the Fathers, including Tertullian, Basil and Chrysostom, condemned it. The Catechism: ‘Since the first century the Church has affirmed the moral evil of every procured abortion. This teaching has not changed and remains unchangeable. Direct abortion, that is to say, abortion willed either as an end or a means, is gravely contrary to the moral law.’ (CCC 2271)”", "Say: “The reasoning. Biology: at fertilization, a new, genetically distinct, living human organism comes into being. It is not a part of the mother’s body; it has its own DNA, sex and development. It develops continuously; there is no point at which it becomes human, though it becomes more developed. Philosophy: the question is not ‘when does it become a person?’ with criteria like consciousness, viability or independence, because these criteria would also exclude newborns, the sleeping, or the severely disabled. The Church argues that personhood is a property of what a thing is, not what it can do. The prudential argument: even if one were unsure, we should not risk killing. Evangelium Vitae (1995) of John Paul II: ‘the unconditional respect for the right to life of every innocent person.’”", "Say: “Responses to hard cases. Rape: a horrific crime; the woman needs compassion and care; the child is innocent, and punishing the child does not heal the crime. Health of the mother: if a pregnant woman has a life-threatening condition, treatment aiming to cure the mother (e.g., removing a cancerous uterus, treating an ectopic pregnancy) is permitted even if the child dies as a side effect. Direct abortion is never allowed. Fetal abnormality: every life has value; perinatal hospice and support exist. Poverty: the Church supports mothers with material help. Legal question: the Church holds that law should protect the unborn and doesn’t see abortion as only a private matter; the debate involves political prudence. Healing: Project Rachel, confession, and compassion. Pro-life means pro-woman and pro-child: Elizabeth and Mary supported each other. Being pro-life also means supporting the elderly, the poor, the disabled, and ending violence.”"],
    ask=["What does the Church teach about abortion? (It is gravely wrong; life begins at conception.)", "What is the difference between abortion and treatment of a cancerous uterus? (Abortion directly intends the child’s death; the treatment aims to cure the mother.)", "What does being pro-life mean fully? (Supporting mothers, children, the disabled and the vulnerable.)"],
    practice=["Make a table: Science / Philosophy / Scripture and Tradition / Church’s conclusion.", "Write the strongest objection you have heard and a charitable reply.", "List five forms of practical support for a woman in a crisis pregnancy.", "Choose one to support concretely."],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary for mothers, unborn children and all affected. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for a mother in a difficult pregnancy.”")),

'12-4-2': dict(
  saint="Aloysius Gonzaga: a Jesuit novice who died at twenty-three caring for plague victims.",
  tryit=["Distinguish ordinary from extraordinary means with cases.", "Describe what accompanying the dying involves.", "Draft your own advance directive values."],
  saintStory="Aloysius Gonzaga was born in 1568, the oldest son of the Marquis of Castiglione, in northern Italy. He was heir to a title and lands, and grew up in a world of courts and military training. As a boy he was thrilled by war games, and picked up bad language from the soldiers, but was ashamed of it. At about nine, he took a private vow of virginity and became devoted to prayer.\nHis father opposed his wish to become a priest, wanting him to be a courtier. After years of struggle, Aloysius gave up his claim to the title to his brother, and in 1585, at seventeen, entered the Jesuit novitiate in Rome. In 1591, plague swept the city. The Jesuits opened a hospital for the sick. Aloysius volunteered, washing patients and carrying them on his shoulders from the streets to the hospital.\nHe caught the plague and was sick for weeks. He died on June 21, 1591, aged twenty-three. He was canonized in 1726 and is patron of youth. His death came from caring for the dying.",
  script=dict(
    open="Be gentle. Say, “Many of us have lost someone. We’ll treat this topic with care.” Ask, “If someone you loved were dying, what would you want for them?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Review the Church’s teaching (CCC 2276-2279; Evangelium Vitae 64-67; Samaritanus Bonus 2020). Those who are ill or disabled must be cared for. Euthanasia, intentionally causing death to relieve suffering, is always wrong. Assisted suicide is wrong. So-called ‘mercy killing’ is a false mercy. But discontinuing medical procedures that are burdensome, dangerous, extraordinary or disproportionate to the expected outcome is legitimate: it is a refusal of ‘over-zealous’ treatment. One may not choose death, but one may accept it. Ordinary care, such as food, water, hygiene, warmth and companionship, owed to every patient, must not be withdrawn merely to cause death. But if they can’t be assimilated or cause grave burden, they may be limited.”", "Say: “The ordinary/extraordinary distinction is about proportion: the benefits versus burdens for this patient. A ventilator for a brief crisis, with hope of recovery, may be ordinary; the same device when the patient is dying and it only prolongs the process, may be disproportionate. Patients (or surrogates) may refuse treatment, with the intent of not prolonging dying, not of causing death. Pain relief: it is permissible to give analgesics even if they might hasten death as a side effect, provided the intention is to relieve pain (double effect). Palliative sedation, used appropriately, for intractable suffering near death, is allowed when not intended to cause death. Brain death and organ donation: the Church accepts the criteria if applied rigorously; organ donation is a generous act, if consent is free and the donor is truly dead.”", "Say: “Advance planning. A health care proxy and an advance directive, preferably consistent with Church teaching, help families: they can state wishes about extraordinary measures and name a trusted decision-maker. The Church encourages Catholics to complete them. ‘Do not resuscitate’ orders and POLST forms: can be morally acceptable in some circumstances but should be reviewed. Accompaniment: the Church’s answer to suffering is presence, not elimination. Families should be supported; hospice care can help. The sacraments: Anointing, Viaticum, Confession. Prayer: the Commendation of the Dying, the Rosary. Forgiveness and farewells. Suffering has meaning in union with Christ’s cross, but it’s no good to romanticize pain. John Paul II’s own death in 2005 was a witness: he asked, ‘Let me go to the house of the Father.’ Aloysius gave his life serving the dying.”"],
    ask=["Is it ever permitted to stop treatment? (Yes, when it is disproportionate or burdensome, with no intent to cause death.)", "What is double effect? (Relieving pain with an unintended risk of shortening life is allowed.)", "What is the Church’s answer to suffering? (Presence, care and the sacraments.)"],
    practice=["Work in groups on cases: a patient on a ventilator with no hope; a patient refusing chemotherapy; a dying patient in pain; a request for assisted suicide.", "For each, decide what the Church would permit and what not.", "Write a short list of values for your own advance directive.", "Plan how you might support a dying relative."],
    close="Pray the Commendation of the Dying or “Jesus, Mary and Joseph, assist me in my last agony.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Talk with your family about end-of-life wishes.”")),

'12-4-3': dict(
  saint="The archangel Raphael: whose name means “God heals,” and who walked beside young Tobias.",
  tryit=["Judge three technologies by the questions the Church asks.", "Mark what serves persons.", "Write a rule you would adopt."],
  saintStory="Raphael is one of the three archangels named in Scripture, with Michael and Gabriel. His name in Hebrew means “God heals.” He appears in the Book of Tobit, in which an older man, Tobit, goes blind, and his son Tobias sets out on a long journey to collect a debt. Tobias hires a companion, who is really Raphael in disguise.\nOn the journey, a large fish leaps out of the river and tries to swallow Tobias. Raphael tells him to catch it, and to keep its heart, liver and gall, which are useful as medicine. In a distant city, they stay with Raguel, whose daughter Sarah has been widowed seven times because a demon killed each of her husbands on the wedding night. Raphael instructs Tobias how to drive away the demon, and Tobias and Sarah marry.\nBack home, Raphael tells Tobias to put the fish’s gall on his father’s eyes, and Tobit regains his sight. Raphael then reveals himself: “I am Raphael, one of the seven angels who stand ready and enter before the glory of the Lord.” “Bless God.” He is the patron of the sick, of physicians and travelers.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Name a medical or technological breakthrough in your lifetime. What good does it do? What new questions does it raise?” Take answers and list on the board.",
    teach=["Say: “Technology is a gift of human intelligence, which participates in God’s creativity. The Church supports medical research and technology that serve human life and dignity. But some things are ethical lines. The Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith’s Donum Vitae (1987) and Dignitas Personae (2008) deal with bioethics. The key principles: (1) every human being is a person from conception, with dignity and rights; (2) the person may never be treated merely as a means; (3) procreation belongs in the marital act of love and shouldn’t be replaced by technique; (4) the end doesn’t justify the means. A good question: not only ‘Can we?’ but ‘Should we?’”", "Say: “Some current issues. Embryonic stem-cell research: destroys human embryos, and is immoral; adult stem cells, cord blood and induced pluripotent stem cells are ethical. Genetic testing: legitimate for diagnosis and therapy; not legitimate for selecting or discarding embryos or children. Gene therapy: acceptable for healing. Genetic enhancement and ‘designer babies’: raises worries of eugenics and inequality. Cloning: reproductive cloning is immoral since it substitutes technique for the act of love and treats the child as a product. Organ transplantation: a good act, provided donors are dead and consent is free; organ trafficking is gravely wrong. Artificial intelligence: raises questions of work, privacy, relationships, truth and war; Pope Francis has called for ‘algor-ethics.’ Transhumanism: the idea of overcoming human limits through technology, risks losing the meaning of the person.”", "Say: “How do we think about new technologies? A method: See (what is happening), Judge (by the Gospel and the Church’s teaching: dignity, common good, solidarity, stewardship), Act. Questions: Does it respect life from conception to natural death? Does it treat the person as a subject rather than an object? Does it serve healing or domination? Who benefits and who is left out? Does it protect the vulnerable? Does it respect the family and the natural meaning of the body? Are the means ethical? Raphael’s name reminds us that healing is God’s work, and medicine cooperates in it. Pope Francis wrote in Laudato Si’ of a technocratic paradigm, which tends to see everything as raw material. The remedy is wisdom, humility and moral reflection.”"],
    ask=["What are the Church’s key bioethics principles? (Dignity from conception; never use persons as means; procreation in marital act; ends don’t justify means.)", "Which stem-cell sources are ethical? (Adult, cord blood, iPS cells; not embryonic.)", "What question should we ask of new technology? (Should we, not only can we.)"],
    practice=["Choose three technologies: embryo screening, AI chat companions, organ transplant.", "Apply five questions to each.", "Decide what you think the Church would say and what guardrails are needed.", "Write a rule you would adopt for your own use of technology."],
    close="Pray the prayer to St. Raphael for healing and guidance. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Discuss one bioethics question with a family member.”")),

'12-5-1': dict(
  saint="Metropolitan Andrey Sheptytsky: a Ukrainian Greek Catholic leader who hid Jews and kept the East’s traditions alive.",
  tryit=["Compare East and West on five points.", "Mark what we share.", "Describe Eastern Catholic Churches."],
  saintStory="Roman Sheptytsky was born in 1865 into an old Ukrainian noble family in Galicia (now western Ukraine). He studied law and served in the Austrian army, then astonished his family by becoming a Basilian monk, taking the name Andrey. In 1901 he became metropolitan archbishop of Lviv for the Ukrainian Greek Catholic Church, which has an Eastern rite but is in communion with Rome. He led for forty-four years.\nHe founded schools, a national museum and a hospital, supported the poor with his own money, and promoted the Church’s Eastern heritage. He also longed for the union of the Eastern Churches with Rome, while respecting the Orthodox. In World War II, he opposed the Nazi occupation. He hid about 150 Jews, including children, in monasteries and in his own residence, and in 1942 wrote a pastoral letter, “Thou Shalt Not Kill,” condemning the murder of Jews. He wrote to Himmler, protesting.\nHe died in 1944. Pope Francis declared him Venerable in 2015. His life shows the Eastern Catholic Church in the 20th century: faithful in persecution, linking East and West.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show an icon and a Western painting side by side. Ask, “What differences do you notice? What do you think they teach about how each tradition prays?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Eastern Christianity includes two groups. The Orthodox Churches, not in communion with the Pope, are about 260 million members: Greek, Russian, Serbian, Romanian, Bulgarian, Georgian and others, led by patriarchs, with the Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople as ‘first among equals.’ The Oriental Orthodox (Coptic, Ethiopian, Armenian, Syriac) separated after the Council of Chalcedon (451). The Eastern Catholic Churches: 23 Churches in communion with Rome with their own rites and laws, such as Ukrainian, Maronite, Melkite, Syro-Malabar, Chaldean, and Ruthenian. About 18 million Catholics. The Latin (Roman) Church is the largest, but one of many. All are fully Catholic. Vatican II’s Orientalium Ecclesiarum affirms their equal dignity.”", "Say: “What the Orthodox and Catholics share: the Nicene Creed (except one word), seven sacraments, valid bishops and priests in apostolic succession, the Real Presence, devotion to Mary (the Theotokos), saints, icons, monasticism, the Fathers, and the first seven ecumenical councils. The main differences: the papacy (Orthodox accept the Pope as bishop of Rome with a primacy of honor, but reject universal jurisdiction and infallibility); the filioque (the Spirit proceeds from the Father ‘and the Son’), a Western addition; some other doctrines such as the Immaculate Conception, purgatory, and divorce and remarriage (Orthodox permit remarriage in certain cases). Style: the East emphasizes mystery, liturgy as heaven on earth, theosis (divinization), and icons; the West, systematic theology, law and activity.”", "Say: “Eastern liturgy: the Divine Liturgy of St. John Chrysostom, with chant, incense and the iconostasis; the sacraments of initiation given together to infants (Baptism, chrismation, Eucharist); married priests; leavened bread. Prayer: the Jesus Prayer, hesychasm. Relations: the Great Schism of 1054; the Fourth Crusade’s sack of Constantinople in 1204; the failed unions of Lyon (1274) and Florence (1439); the Union of Brest (1596). Contemporary: Paul VI and Athenagoras lifted the excommunications in 1965; John Paul II’s ‘two lungs’; the Joint Declaration with Patriarch Kirill (2016). Challenges: Ukraine, where Eastern Catholics and Orthodox have both suffered. The Church prays for full communion. What can Western Catholics do? Learn about the East, attend an Eastern liturgy, read the Eastern Fathers, and pray for unity.”"],
    ask=["How many Eastern Catholic Churches are there? (23.)", "Name two differences between Orthodox and Catholic belief. (The papacy and the filioque.)", "What is the iconostasis? (The icon screen separating the nave from the sanctuary in Eastern churches.)"],
    practice=["Make a Venn diagram of Orthodox / Catholic / shared.", "Fill in at least eight items in the overlap.", "Write three things you admire in the Eastern tradition.", "Plan a visit to an Eastern church or reading of an Eastern author."],
    close="Pray the Trisagion: “Holy God, Holy Mighty, Holy Immortal, have mercy on us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Learn how the Divine Liturgy begins.”")),

'12-5-2': dict(
  saint="Dietrich Bonhoeffer: a Lutheran pastor hanged by the Nazis, honored by Catholics among the martyrs of the twentieth century.",
  tryit=["Compare Catholic and Protestant positions on five points.", "State what we share.", "Plan a respectful conversation."],
  saintStory="Dietrich Bonhoeffer was born in 1906 in Breslau, Germany, into a family of scholars. He studied theology at Berlin and Union Theological Seminary in New York, where he attended the Abyssinian Baptist Church in Harlem and was struck by its faith. He became a Lutheran pastor and teacher. When Hitler came to power in 1933, he opposed the Nazi interference in the church and helped found the Confessing Church. He led an underground seminary at Finkenwalde and wrote The Cost of Discipleship, arguing against “cheap grace.”\nHe could have stayed safe in the United States, but returned to Germany in 1939, saying he must share the trials of his people. He joined the resistance and helped Jews escape. He was arrested in April 1943. In prison, he wrote letters and poems, later published as Letters and Papers from Prison. He was implicated in a plot against Hitler.\nHe was hanged at Flossenbürg on April 9, 1945, just before the Allies arrived. His last words reportedly: “This is the end — for me the beginning of life.” John Paul II included him in the ecumenical commemoration of twentieth-century martyrs in 2000.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What do you know about Protestants? What do you admire about them? What do you find confusing?” Take answers, and note misconceptions.",
    teach=["Say: “Protestant communities arose from the sixteenth-century Reformation. About 900 million people. Main families: Lutherans (from Martin Luther, in Germany and Scandinavia); Reformed and Presbyterian (from Calvin and Zwingli); Anglicans (the Church of England and Anglican Communion); Baptists; Methodists; Pentecostals and charismatics (the fastest-growing); and thousands of independent churches. They share belief in the Trinity, the divinity of Christ, the authority of Scripture, and salvation through Christ. Vatican II (Unitatis Redintegratio 3) says those who believe in Christ and are baptized are ‘in some, though imperfect, communion with the Catholic Church’ and have ‘a right to be called Christians’ and ‘are properly regarded as brothers and sisters in the Lord.’”", "Say: “The Reformation principles: sola scriptura (Scripture alone), sola fide (faith alone), sola gratia (grace alone), the priesthood of all believers. Catholic response: Scripture is the inspired word of God, and also needs Tradition and the Church to be interpreted; faith and works are related; sacraments are real means of grace; the Church has a visible structure with bishops and the Pope. Differences: the number of sacraments (most Protestants recognize two, Baptism and the Lord’s Supper); the Eucharist (Lutherans hold a real presence; Reformed varied; many see it as symbolic); the papacy; Mary and the saints; the canon (Protestants omit the deuterocanonical books); clergy and celibacy; authority and Tradition; the Church’s visibility.”", "Say: “Progress and cooperation. Official dialogues: Catholic-Lutheran Joint Declaration on Justification (1999); Anglican-Roman Catholic International Commission (ARCIC); with Methodists and Reformed. Shared witness: pro-life work, charity, Bible societies, hymns and prayer. Catholic respect for Protestants: gratitude for their devotion to Scripture, evangelistic zeal, and hymnody. Cautions: avoid indifferentism (‘it doesn’t matter what we believe’) and avoid proselytizing. Marriage: mixed marriages need permission; Communion: usually not shared across churches because it signifies full unity. Bonhoeffer’s story shows the shared witness of martyrdom. Tips for respectful conversation: listen first; ask what they believe, don’t assume; know your own faith; focus on common ground first; avoid caricatures; pray together; and remember that many have been hurt by division.”"],
    ask=["Name the four Reformation ‘solas’ listed. (Scripture, faith, grace, and the priesthood of all believers.)", "What does the Church call baptized Protestants? (Brothers and sisters in the Lord, in imperfect communion.)", "Why is Communion not shared across churches? (It signifies full unity of faith and communion.)"],
    practice=["Make a three-column chart: What we share / Where we differ / How to talk.", "Fill in at least five items in each.", "Prepare three good questions to ask a Protestant friend.", "Plan an ecumenical action: shared service, prayer or Bible study."],
    close="Pray for Christian unity: “That they may all be one.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Invite a friend of another denomination to a shared service project.”")),

'12-5-3': dict(
  saint="Blessed Christian de Chergé and the monks of Tibhirine: seven French monks who stayed in Algeria among Muslim neighbors, and were killed in 1996.",
  tryit=["Describe what we share with Judaism and Islam.", "Describe where we differ.", "Write a rule for interfaith friendship."],
  saintStory="In the 1990s, a civil war raged in Algeria between the government and Islamist militants. At the Trappist monastery of Our Lady of the Atlas in Tibhirine, in the mountains, nine monks lived and prayed among their Muslim neighbors, running a small clinic and farm. The prior, Christian de Chergé, had been an army officer in Algeria in the 1950s, where an Algerian friend, a Muslim named Mohammed, saved his life, and later died protecting him. That deep friendship shaped his vocation.\nWhen the threats grew, the monks were told to leave. They voted to stay, out of solidarity with the villagers. On the night of March 26-27, 1996, armed men kidnapped seven monks. Two months later they were found dead.\nChristian de Chergé had written a testament, to be opened in the event of his death. He wrote that he forgave in advance his killer, “my last-minute friend,” and prayed that they would meet in paradise “like happy good thieves.” He was beatified in 2018 with eighteen other martyrs of Algeria. The Muslims of Tibhirine mourned them.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What do you know about Jews, Muslims, Hindus and Buddhists? Where did you learn it? How accurate do you think it is?” Take answers and correct misinformation gently.",
    teach=["Say: “Nostra Aetate (1965) is the Church’s declaration on relations with non-Christian religions. It affirms: ‘The Catholic Church rejects nothing that is true and holy in these religions. She regards with sincere reverence those ways of conduct and of life, those precepts and teachings which, though differing in many particulars from what she holds and sets forth, nonetheless often reflect a ray of that Truth which enlightens all men.’ At the same time, the Church ‘proclaims, and is duty bound to proclaim without fail, Christ who is ‘the way, the truth, and the life’ (John 14:6).’ Dialogue doesn’t mean giving up conviction, but listening and respecting.”", "Say: “Judaism: the Church’s elder brother. Christianity is rooted in Israel. The Old Testament is our Scripture. Jesus, Mary and the apostles were Jews. The Church condemns antisemitism and holds that the Jewish people are not guilty of Jesus’ death, and that God’s covenant with them is irrevocable (Romans 11:29). John Paul II called Jews ‘our dearly beloved brothers’ and ‘our elder brothers.’ Differences: Jews do not accept Jesus as Messiah. Islam: Muslims worship the one God, merciful and almighty, creator of heaven and earth. They honor Jesus as a prophet (not God), revere Mary, and fast and give alms. Differences: Trinity, Incarnation, Jesus’ crucifixion, the authority of the Qur’an and Muhammad. The Council urged mutual understanding. Hinduism and Buddhism: they seek answers to the great questions; the Church encourages respect and sees ‘seeds of the Word.’”", "Say: “Principles. One: human dignity and religious freedom. Two: truth is found wherever it is, but fullness in Christ. Three: the Church’s mission remains, but evangelization respects freedom and is done in love and example. Four: cooperation on shared goods: peace, poverty, life, family. The Assisi gathering (1986). The 2019 Document on Human Fraternity. Practical: learn about neighbors’ faith from them; visit a mosque or synagogue; welcome neighbors; avoid stereotypes; speak honestly about differences; do not syncretize (mixing). The Tibhirine monks lived dialogue in daily friendship. Dialogue of life, action, theological exchange and spiritual experience. Our own faith deepens when we meet others with openness and clarity.”"],
    ask=["What does Nostra Aetate say about other religions? (The Church rejects nothing true and holy in them.)", "What is the Church’s view of Jews today? (Elder brothers; the covenant is irrevocable; antisemitism is condemned.)", "What do Muslims share with Christians? (Worship of one God, honor for Jesus as prophet and Mary.)"],
    practice=["Make a table: Judaism / Islam / Hinduism / Buddhism: shared beliefs, key differences, one respectful practice.", "Write three rules for interfaith friendship.", "Choose one neighbor or classmate of another faith and plan a friendly conversation.", "Write a prayer for peace among the faiths."],
    close="Pray for peace among believers. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Have a respectful conversation with someone of another faith.”")),

'12-6-1': dict(
  saint="Edmund Campion: a brilliant Oxford scholar who came secretly to England to argue for the faith and died for it.",
  tryit=["Answer “Where does the Bible say that?” with three sources.", "Build a short argument for the Church’s role.", "Write the reply to sola scriptura."],
  saintStory="Edmund Campion was born in London in 1540, and became a star at Oxford. Queen Elizabeth I visited the university in 1566, and Campion, then a young scholar, delivered a speech in front of her. He was ordained an Anglican deacon, but became troubled by doubts. By 1571, he was convinced of the Catholic faith and fled to Douai, in France, where he was received back. He joined the Jesuits in 1573.\nIn 1580, he returned secretly to England, which was then dangerous for priests: it was treason to be a Catholic priest. Disguised as a jewel merchant, he traveled the country, saying Mass and hearing confessions. He wrote a letter of challenge to the Privy Council, “Campion’s Brag,” offering to debate any scholar, and wrote a book, Ten Reasons (Rationes Decem), defending the faith by arguments from Scripture and history. It was printed secretly and left on the benches of Oxford’s church.\nHe was caught in July 1581, tortured, and forced to debate in the Tower. In December, he was condemned and hanged, drawn and quartered at Tyburn, on December 1, 1581. He was canonized in 1970 among the Forty Martyrs of England and Wales.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If a friend said, ‘Show me where the Bible says you should listen to the Church,’ what would you say? What if they said, ‘The Bible alone is enough’?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Apologetics means giving reasons for faith. 1 Peter 3:15: ‘Always be prepared to make a defense to anyone who calls you to account for the hope that is in you; yet do it with gentleness and reverence.’ Tone matters. First, listen: understand what the person actually believes. Second, find common ground. Third, respond with reasons, clearly. Fourth, be humble. The goal is to win the person, not the argument. Many objections come from misunderstandings of Catholic teaching, or from real wounds. Apologetics clears the way; the Holy Spirit converts.”", "Say: “Sola scriptura: the claim that the Bible alone is the authority. Catholic reply. One: the Bible does not say it. 2 Timothy 3:16 says Scripture is ‘profitable’ but doesn’t say ‘only.’ Two: the Bible itself points to Tradition (2 Thessalonians 2:15; 2 Timothy 2:2; John 21:25) and to the Church as ‘the pillar and bulwark of the truth’ (1 Timothy 3:15). Three: the New Testament canon was determined by the Church. Four: the Bible requires interpretation; Protestants disagree widely, resulting in tens of thousands of denominations. Five: the early Church had no Bible as we know it for decades, and was guided by apostolic teaching. Six: Jesus established a Church with authority (Matthew 16, 18). This isn’t against Scripture but about how Scripture is read. Catholics honor Scripture: they read it at every Mass.”", "Say: “Other common questions on Scripture and authority. ‘Why do Catholics have extra books?’ The Septuagint, used by Jesus’ contemporaries, included them; Luther’s removal followed a later Jewish canon. ‘Why do you call priests Father?’ Jesus’ warning (Matthew 23:9) is against pride; Paul calls himself spiritual father (1 Corinthians 4:15). ‘Is the Pope in the Bible?’ Matthew 16:18-19; John 21:15-17; Luke 22:32. ‘What about tradition of men?’ Mark 7:8 condemns human traditions that contradict God’s word, not apostolic Tradition. Method: use the Bible and history, and always charity. Campion’s approach was clear and respectful, and he stayed calm in the face of death. The witness of life is the strongest apologetic.”"],
    ask=["What does apologetics mean? (Giving reasons for faith.)", "What does 1 Peter 3:15 say about tone? (With gentleness and reverence.)", "Give one Catholic reply to sola scriptura. (The Bible doesn’t teach it; the Church determined the canon; Tradition is in Scripture.)"],
    practice=["Choose three common objections and write a short, courteous reply with Scripture for each.", "Practice with a partner: one asks, the other answers in 60 seconds.", "Evaluate: Did the answer listen first? Was it charitable?", "Write one question you want to find an answer to."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give me the right words and a gentle heart.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Learn one good Scripture argument well.”")),

'12-6-2': dict(
  saint="Venerable Fulton Sheen: an archbishop who explained the faith to millions on television and prayed an hour before the Eucharist every day.",
  tryit=["Answer an objection about the Eucharist using John 6 and the Fathers.", "Answer an objection about praying to saints.", "Evaluate the tone."],
  saintStory="Fulton Sheen was born in 1895 in Illinois, and was ordained a priest in 1919. He earned a doctorate in philosophy in Belgium, was a professor at Catholic University, and a gifted preacher. He promised at ordination to spend a holy hour daily before the Blessed Sacrament, and did so for the next sixty years.\nIn 1951 he began a TV program, Life Is Worth Living. Wearing a cape, standing in front of a blackboard with no script, he spoke about faith, culture, family and communism in clear language. Tens of millions watched; he won an Emmy in 1952. He was consecrated bishop in 1951, and directed the Society for the Propagation of the Faith, giving away the money from his broadcasts to the missions. He later became bishop of Rochester, New York, and an archbishop.\nHe converted many through his talks, including notable figures. He died in 1979. He was declared Venerable in 2012. He said, “There are not one hundred people in America who hate the Catholic Church, but there are millions who hate what they wrongly think the Catholic Church is.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “A friend says, ‘You think a piece of bread is God, and you pray to dead people. Isn’t that weird?’ How would you answer without getting defensive?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “On the Eucharist. Objection: ‘It’s only a symbol.’ Reply: John 6. Jesus says, ‘My flesh is food indeed, and my blood is drink indeed.’ (6:55) He uses a strong verb for ‘eat’ (trogein, ‘gnaw’). When many disciples left, he did not say, ‘You misunderstood, I was speaking symbolically.’ He let them go. At the Last Supper he said, ‘This is my body,’ not ‘This represents.’ Paul: those who receive unworthily are ‘guilty of the body and blood of the Lord.’ (1 Corinthians 11:27) The early Church: Ignatius of Antioch (AD 107) said heretics ‘abstain from the Eucharist because they do not confess that the Eucharist is the flesh of our Savior Jesus Christ.’ Justin Martyr (AD 155): it is not common bread. No Christian writer for a thousand years taught that it was merely a symbol.”", "Say: “On the saints. Objection: ‘Praying to saints takes away from Jesus; Scripture says there’s one mediator (1 Timothy 2:5).’ Reply: We ask saints for prayers, as we ask each other. Paul asked for prayers (Romans 15:30). If it’s fine to ask a friend on earth to pray, why not one in heaven? The dead in Christ are alive: ‘He is not God of the dead, but of the living.’ (Matthew 22:32) Revelation 5:8 and 6:9-10 shows saints in heaven offering prayers and interceding. Hebrews 12:1: ‘a cloud of witnesses.’ We don’t worship saints; we honor them. We do not take away from Christ; the saints are his members. On Mary: Luke 1:48, ‘All generations will call me blessed.’ On statues: not idols but reminders, as photographs.”", "Say: “Other common objections. ‘Why confess to a priest?’ John 20:23. ‘Why call the Eucharist a sacrifice?’ Malachi 1:11; Hebrews 13:10; 1 Corinthians 10:16-18. ‘Mass is boring repetition.’ Matthew 6:7 condemns empty babbling, not prayer; the Mass is rich in Scripture. Tone: Sheen said, ‘Hate the error, love the person who holds it.’ Be willing to say ‘I don’t know, I’ll find out.’ Use the Catechism and good resources. Pray before a conversation. After the conversation, pray for the person. Best apologetic: a life of joy, charity and integrity, and an invitation to ‘come and see’ (John 1:46). Sheen’s holy hour was the secret behind his preaching.”"],
    ask=["What does John 6 teach? (Jesus’ flesh and blood are real food and drink.)", "Why do Catholics ask saints to pray? (Same reason we ask friends; they are alive in Christ.)", "What was Sheen’s daily practice? (A holy hour before the Blessed Sacrament.)"],
    practice=["Pair up. One plays the skeptic, one the Catholic, using the Eucharist or saints objection.", "The Catholic must listen, affirm common ground, give a Scripture reason, and invite a conversation.", "Swap roles.", "Debrief: what worked, what felt defensive?"],
    close="Pray quietly before the Blessed Sacrament if possible, or: “Jesus, I adore you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Make a holy hour or a half-hour of adoration this month.”")),

'12-6-3': dict(
  saint="Callistus I: a former slave and convict who became pope and taught that the Church can forgive.",
  tryit=["Build a case for Peter’s role from three texts.", "Build a case for priestly absolution.", "Prepare replies to two objections."],
  saintStory="Callistus was born a slave in Rome in the second century. His master, Carpophorus, a Christian, gave him money to set up a bank for Christian widows and others. The bank failed, and Callistus fled but was caught. Some accounts say he was beaten and sent to a treadmill; later he was sentenced to the mines of Sardinia for a disturbance. He was freed through the intervention of a Christian woman close to the emperor’s household.\nPope Zephyrinus made him a deacon in charge of the Christian cemetery on the Appian Way, which is still called the Catacombs of Callistus. He became bishop of Rome in about 217. He taught that the Church could forgive even grave sins like adultery after penance, which scandalized rigorists such as Hippolytus, who thought it was too lax. Callistus held that the Church is not a society of the perfect but includes the wheat and the weeds together.\nHe was martyred around 222, tradition says thrown from a window in Trastevere. His pontificate marks an early assertion of the Church’s power to forgive.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If a Protestant friend asked, ‘Why do you need a priest to forgive your sins? And why do you need a Pope at all?’ what would your answer be?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Confession. Objections: ‘Only God can forgive sins.’ Reply: True; and God has given the power to the apostles. John 20:21-23: ‘As the Father has sent me, even so I send you… If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven.’ Matthew 18:18: binding and loosing. 2 Corinthians 5:18: ‘God… gave us the ministry of reconciliation.’ James 5:16: ‘confess your sins to one another.’ Mark 2:7-10: Jesus forgave sins, and Matthew 9:8 says God gave such authority ‘to men.’ How would the apostles know whether to forgive or retain sins unless they heard them? The early Church practiced confession (Didache; Tertullian; Cyprian; Origen). Callistus’s assertion. The benefit: certainty of forgiveness, counsel, healing, and the support of the Church.”", "Say: “The papacy. Objections: ‘Peter was a man, not a pope’; ‘the rock is Peter’s faith’; ‘the Pope is not in the Bible.’ Replies. Matthew 16:18-19: Jesus gives Simon a new name, Rock, and the keys, which in Isaiah 22:22 represent the office of the master of the household, who ‘shall open, and none shall shut.’ Peter is always first in lists of apostles (Matthew 10:2), speaks first at Pentecost, leads at the Council of Jerusalem, and Jesus prays for him in Luke 22:32 and commands him in John 21:15-17. The early Church recognized Rome’s primacy: Clement of Rome intervened in Corinth (AD 96); Ignatius wrote of Rome ‘presiding in love’; Irenaeus (AD 180) said all churches must agree with Rome ‘on account of its preeminent authority.’ Succession lists of popes go back to Peter. The Pope is the Vicar of Christ and servant of the servants of God.”", "Say: “Objections to the papacy: bad popes in history (the Church admits sins; Judas was an apostle); infallibility (limited to definitions on faith and morals); Eastern Christians disagree about jurisdiction; the Reformation. Reply: Jesus promised that the gates of hell will not prevail; the papacy is a gift of unity. Unity needs a visible center, as nations need a constitution. A balanced presentation: the Pope is a successor of Peter, with real authority but under the word of God. Papal primacy is not meant to crush the bishops’ role but to serve unity. Pope Francis’s emphasis on the Pope as pastor and servant. We pray for the Pope at every Mass. Callistus reminds us that the Church includes sinners, and the Church’s power to forgive is part of its mission.”"],
    ask=["What Scripture shows Jesus gave the apostles authority to forgive sins? (John 20:21-23.)", "What do the keys of Matthew 16 recall? (The steward’s office in Isaiah 22.)", "Which early witnesses affirm Rome’s primacy? (Clement, Ignatius, Irenaeus.)"],
    practice=["Make two argument maps: Confession and the papacy, each with Scripture, early Church and reason.", "List the two strongest objections and replies for each.", "Practice a 90-second summary of one argument.", "Evaluate each other’s tone."],
    close="Pray for the Pope and the Church’s leaders. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read Matthew 16:13-20 and John 20:19-23 slowly.”")),
}
